Complete Example:
import java.util.*;

public class StreamApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        numbers.add(5);
        numbers.add(9);
        numbers.add(8);
        numbers.add(8);
        numbers.add(1);

        // Print original list
        numbers.forEach((n) -&gt; System.out.print(n));
        System.out.println();  // Output: 59881

        // Apply stream operations
        numbers.stream()
            .filter(n -&gt; n &gt; 5)        // Keep only numbers &gt; 5: [9, 8, 8]
            .distinct()                // Remove duplicates: [9, 8]
            .sorted()                  // Sort: [8, 9]
            .limit(1)                  // Take first element: [8]
            .forEach(System.out::print);

        System.out.println("\n" + numbers);
    }
}
Output:
59881
8
[5, 9, 8, 8, 1]
Step-by-step breakdown:

Original list: [5, 9, 8, 8, 1]
After filter(n -&gt; n &gt; 5): [9, 8, 8] (only elements greater than 5)
After distinct(): [9, 8] (duplicates removed)
After sorted(): [8, 9] (sorted in ascending order)
After limit(1): [8] (only first element)
forEach(System.out::print): Prints 8

Important Note: The original list numbers remains unchanged [5, 9, 8, 8, 1] because stream operations don’t modify the source.
Extended Example with collect():
List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers = Arrays.asList(5, 9, 8, 8, 1);

